WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.220
A space station in a low orbit goes once
around the Earth in about 90 minutes.

2
00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:10.030
The Moon, much more
distant, takes 27.3 days.

3
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.520
Both are held by a single
force, Earth's gravity,

4
00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.060
and neither orbit depends on
the orbiting object's mass.

5
00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:20.800
So how does the time for one
orbit depend on its size?

6
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.280
Double the radius: how many
times longer does one orbit take?

7
00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.450
In this mission, you'll see what keeps
a satellite moving on its circle.

8
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.630
Then you'll use the Moon's
orbit to weigh the Earth.

9
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:38.030
Your mission: find out how many times longer
one orbit takes when the radius doubles.
